        "original_id": "ccd9ca2e-f08e-5e27-bbdb-ed92491c8cf7",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Stefan Edberg, Édouard Roger-Vasselin, Julien Benneteau, Brad Gilbert\n- Events: 1984 unofficial Olympic tournament, 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships – Singles, Roland Garros 2014\n- Topics: tennis rankings, Grand Slam titles, Davis Cup, serve-and-volley\n\nFacts:\n- Stefan Edberg won the unofficial Olympic tennis tournament in 1984.\n- Stefan Edberg won six Grand Slam singles titles and three Grand Slam men's doubles titles between 1985 and 1996.\n- Stefan Edberg was part of four Swedish Davis Cup-winning teams.\n- Stefan Edberg won the Masters Grand Prix, four Masters Series titles, and four Championship Series titles.\n- Stefan Edberg was ranked in the singles top 10 for ten consecutive years and in the top 5 for nine years.\n- Stefan Edberg, born on 19 January 1966, is a Swedish former world No. 1 professional tennis player in both singles and doubles and was a major proponent of serve-and-volley tennis.\n- Stefan Edberg is considered one of the greatest tennis players of his era.\n- At the 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships – Singles, defending champion Brad Gilbert lost to Stefan Edberg 6–1, 6–1 in the final.\n- Édouard Roger-Vasselin won the 2014 Roland Garros men's doubles title with Julien Benneteau.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "94db79a0-1d9a-54d5-9467-418d62c08f81",
        "text": "Page: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: Stefan Bengt Edberg (] ; born 19 January 1966) is a Swedish former world no. 1 professional tennis player (in both singles and doubles).\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: A major proponent of the serve-and-volley style of tennis, he won six Grand Slam singles titles and three Grand Slam men's doubles titles between 1985 and 1996.\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: He also won the Masters Grand Prix and was a part of the Swedish Davis Cup-winning-team four times.\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: In addition he won four Masters Series titles, four Championship Series titles and the unofficial Olympic tournament 1984, was ranked in the singles top 10 for ten successive years, 9 years in the top 5, and is considered one of the greatest players of his era.\nPage: Édouard Roger-Vasselin. Sentence: He won the men's doubles title at Roland Garros in 2014, partnering Julien Benneteau.\nPage: 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships – Singles. Sentence: Brad Gilbert was the defending champion but Stefan Edberg defeated him 6–1, 6–1 in the final.",
